00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:59.270
When Moana directors Ron Clements and John
Musker decided they wanted to make a movie

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:03.350
based in the Pacific Islands, they began by
researching the mythology of the region, in

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.590
particular the legends of the demi-god Maui.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.459
Indeed, the original title of the film was
going to be "Mighty Maui" and it was going

00:01:09.459 --> 00:01:14.310
to adapt a number of Maui myths into a brand
new story that focussed principally on his

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:15.310
character.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:19.939
One of the main legends they planned to use
involved a sidekick female character who asked

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:22.659
Maui to help rescue her lover who'd been kidnapped.

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:26.859
However, after directors Ron Clements and
John Musker travelled to the Pacific to learn

00:01:26.859 --> 00:01:31.420
more about the culture, they found that their
original idea was rather stereotyped.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:35.810
During their visit, they learned about Oceanic
people's very strong connection to the sea

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:40.409
and their shared history of navigation, so
they decided the story should focus more significantly

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:41.509
on that.

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:45.810
On their return, Clements and Musker tossed
out the "Mighty Maui" concept and reversed

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:50.579
the two character roles, making Maui the sidekick
and Moana the lead character.

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:55.139
Their idea was that the film should concentrate
on being a discovery of who you truly are,

00:01:55.139 --> 00:01:59.869
and they envisaged Moana and Maui as a kind
of True Grit-style odd couple.

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:04.539
As the filmmakers went about enhancing Moana's
role in the movie, they also reduced Maui's

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:05.539
role.

00:02:05.539 --> 00:02:08.400
However, they got to a point where they decided
they needed a little more balance.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.300
For example, "You're Welcome" was originally
going to be Moana's song.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.510
The idea being that when she met Maui on his
island, he would be kind of miserable, so

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:21.830
to raise his spirits, Moana would sing the
song to remind him of all his incredible exploits.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:26.280
In the end, of course, the song was reworked
into a fantastic bragging session for Maui

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.920
about how amazing he thinks he is, with Dwayne
Johnson giving a brilliant rendition of "You're

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.850
Welcome" in the final movie.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:37.420
And to ensure that Maui still made an impact
as a character, the demi-god also went through

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.200
a number of redesigns.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.260
Initially, Maui was going to be much smaller
and rather a disappointment to Moana when

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.720
she first met him.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.340
He was also going to be bald and more of a
grumpy curmudgeon in many early versions.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:54.160
However, after discussions with Pacific Islanders,
the filmmakers found that the Maui of legend

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:57.620
was a much larger-than-life character with
a thick mane of hair.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:02.250
According to the movie's head of story, David
Pimentel, the feedback from Disney's Oceanic

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:06.790
Story Trust was that Maui "needs to be a hero,
almost like Superman."

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:10.590
In particular, Maui had to be given a lot
more hair as that's the source of much of

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.050
his legendary power.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:16.570
Now, one of the most beautiful and touching
moments in Moana happens towards the end of

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:21.970
the film when Moana parts the sea and walks
up to Te Ka to restore her missing heart.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:26.490
Instead of a villain being defeated, what
we see is a peaceful resolution and redemption

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.740
for Te Ka, and, of course, a triumph for Moana.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.910
But in previous versions of the script, this
wasn't what happened; rather, it was Maui

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:39.460
who dominated the end of the story, completely
overshadowing Moana's journey and achievement.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:44.510
In one alternate ending, Moana had to dive
underwater to put the heart back in Te Ka,

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.080
and while doing this, Moana became trapped.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.890
And she was only saved when Maui pulled up
the island with his fishhook.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:55.780
And another alternate ending had Moana and
Maui fighting Te Ka together, but it was Maui

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.790
who was mostly responsible for defeating the
lava demon.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:02.660
Quite late in the day, these endings were
eventually rejected as they diminished Moana's

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:06.350
achievement and made Maui the hero of Moana's
story.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:09.090
The final ending I thought was much more satisfying.

